  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1140 AM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nAn upper level high will be centered over Arizona/New\u00a0Mexico \ntoday with ridging extending northward all the way to Alberta. \nThis will lead to a northwest flow aloft over Colorado. Outflow \nfrom thunderstorms last night and this\u00a0morning increased low level\nmoisture but also cooled the airmass. Temperatures are slow to \nwarm this morning, with 11 AM readings only in the mid to upper \n70s. DEN ACARS soundings still show a strong inversion centered \naround 700mb. Forecast model soundings show this as well, but they\ndon't appear strong enough regarding the inversion. This leads to\na highly uncertain thunderstorm forecast and severe threat for \nlater today. MLCAPE is still forecasted to climb to 1000-2000 J/kg\nalong and east of the I-25 corridor. The 0-6km bulk shear reaches\n30-40 knots. The 30 knots is borderline for severe storms, but \nwhere we see east to southeast surface winds around 10 knots, the \nbulk shear will be closer to 40 knots. This favors areas east of a\nline from Fort Collins to DIA for severe storms. Because of the \nslower heating, storm development will likely be delayed with late\nafternoon\u00a0and early evening hours being favored. Again, given the\nstrength of the inversion, the severe thunderstorm threat remains\nhighly uncertain.\u00a0\n\nThe upper-level high will remain centered to our southeast, but \nthe ridge axis shifts eastward\u00a0over Colorado on Thursday. This \nwill bring warmer temperatures and some drying. Precipitable water\nvalues\u00a0fall below an inch throughout the area, except for the far\neastern plains. Warmer air aloft, less moisture and instability \nwill lead to a notable decrease in thunderstorm activity. There \nshould be enough lingering moisture for isolated high-based \nshowers and thunderstorms. Any rainfall is expected to be light, \nwith gusty outflow winds up to 45 mph possible. However, southeast\nwinds will prevail over far eastern Colorado, helping to preserve\nlow level moisture. MLCAPE will reach 1000-2000 J/kg with 0-6km \nbulk shear of around 40 knots, which would favor severe \nthunderstorms. Warmer air aloft may cap convection, though. \nConvergence along a boundary is likely needed for storms to \ninitially\u00a0form, and models generally don't show this happening.\u00a0\n\nThe high intensifies (slightly) and drifts northeastward for \nFriday. Temperatures warm further, with highs expected to reach \nthe lower to mid 90s across northeast Colorado. Models hint at a \nslight increase in moisture, which should fuel a round of \nafternoon/evening showers and thunderstorms. Given the warm \ntemperatures and limited moisture and instability, the \nshowers/storms are expected to be high-based with gusty outflows \nbeing common.\n\nFor the weekend, an upper level trough (eventually strengthening \ninto a closed low)\u00a0will track east across the northwest part of \nthe country. This will push the upper level high east of the \nregion, with southwest flow aloft developing over Colorado. We'll \nsee an increase in monsoon moisture leading to a better chance for\nshowers and thunderstorms.\u00a0 The very warm air hangs on for \nSaturday with lower to mid 90s expected again, but temperatures \nwill cool to the mid 80s to lower 90s for Sunday.\u00a0\n\nThe weather pattern for next week starts to look like a late\nsummer/early fall pattern, featuring stronger west to southwest\nflow aloft with one or two shortwave troughs passing across the\nregion. For now, models show enough moisture each day for a round\nof showers and thunderstorms. However, wouldn't be surprised if we\nend up with a dry and breezy day due to subsidence behind an\nexiting shortwave trough.",
